#{[17592186563727 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to 0}{t}^{2} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}{e}^{t} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{0}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{0} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the third component is a special limit.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}1 \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{2t-1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{2(0)-1}{{0}^{2}+1})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563729 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\,(6-{t}^{3}),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\,({t}^{2}-6),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\frac{16}{{t}^{3}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6-{2}^{3},{2}^{2}-6,\\frac{16}{{2}^{3}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,2⟩ \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 2}g(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\,(5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-t)),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\,\\sqrt{6-t},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\,2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2),\\sqrt{6-2},2(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)\\times g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)\\times {lim}_{t\\to 2}g(t) \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,2⟩\\times ⟨5,2,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 2)\\,i-((-2)\\cdot 4-5\\cdot 2)\\,j+((-2)\\cdot 2-5\\cdot (-2))\\,k \\\\  & =-12\\,i+18\\,j+6\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-12,18,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563732 :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186563732 :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 8\\end{array}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ {x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=3\\cdot 2=6.$"] [17592186563749 :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, ${a}_{3}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186563749 :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=3 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=18 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{3}=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -8 & 18 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 & 9\\end{array}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 & 9\\end{array}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=3 \\\\ {x}_{2}=6 \\\\ {x}_{3}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=3.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(3)=3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=9.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{a}_{1}+6{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ 9\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+6\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=9\\cdot 6\\cdot 3=162.$"] [17592186563751 :question/problem "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{t} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t-2 \\\\ \\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563751 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to 0}3{e}^{t} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{0} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\ \\frac{{0}^{2}+9}{0+3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\,(t-2) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}{2}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0-2 \\\\ \\frac{3(0)-2}{{0}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{0}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3(-2)+0(2)+3(1) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563754 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s),$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ (1+5s)^{1-s}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186563754 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1/(2s)}.\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} & =\\sqrt{5-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s}=\\frac{{e}^{1-1}}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1-s} & =(1+5\\cdot 1)^{1-1} \\\\  & ={6}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186563755 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$*"] [17592186563755 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$"] [17592186563971 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563978 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563985 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186563998 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186564004 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186564041 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186564048 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186564057 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186564085 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186564121 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186564134 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186564139 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186564176 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186564186 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186564190 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186564221 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186564235 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186564310 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186564321 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186564340 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186564480 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}-{t}^{2}+3t-2 \\\\ -4t+8\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,-\n$$\n{{field-2}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}-t+1 \\\\ 2t-3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186564480 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}-t+1 \\\\ 2t-3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{0} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2(0)}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{0}^{2}-0+1 \\\\ 2(0)-3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+1 \\\\ {c}_{2}-3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+1=2 \\\\ {c}_{2}-3=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=1.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{2}=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}-t+1 \\\\ 2t-3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186564481 :question/problem "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n$$\n\\displaystyle {a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186564481 :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=2{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}-2{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{a}_{1}+(-1){a}_{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=-1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."] [17592186564483 :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-6y-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-6y-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4-2x & -6-6y\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4-2x=0 \\\\ -6-6y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n4-2x=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n-6-6y=0\\,⟹\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(2,-1)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-2x)=-2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-6-6y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-2x)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-6-6y)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(2,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(2,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(2,-1)=det({f}^{″}(2,-1))=(-2)\\cdot (-6)-{0}^{2}=12>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)=({f}^{″}(2,-1))_{11}=-2<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(2,-1).$"] [17592186564485 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 1-x\\end{bmatrix},\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $x+y=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (0)+(0) & =1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0+1 \\\\ 1+0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\notin L\n$$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (1)+(1) & =1 \\\\ 2 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""] [17592186564487 :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -13\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nIII. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solution."] [17592186564493 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ -3x+4\\end{bmatrix},\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $y=-3x+4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+4 \\\\ (0) & =-3(0)+4 \\\\ 0 & =4\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example,$$\n\\displaystyle a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0+1 \\\\ 4+1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\notin L\n$$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+4 \\\\ (5) & =-3(1)+4 \\\\ 5 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""] [17592186564495 :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & \\frac{x}{y}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186564495 :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & \\frac{x}{y}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\n \nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{1}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ is an stationary point of $f(x,y).$  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=det({f}^{″}(0,1))={0}^{2}-1\\cdot 1-=-1<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,1)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,1).$"] [17592186564499 :question/problem "Let\n$$\n\\displaystyle T=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto"] [17592186564501 :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} & -\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}-6{t}^{2}+\\frac{41}{3}t-8 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-\\frac{13}{3}t+5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n$\\,\\,{t}^{2}+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n$\\,\\,t+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}\n  </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n    .\n  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$-2$\n  </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186564501 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-6{t}^{2}+\\frac{41}{3}t-8 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-\\frac{13}{3}t+5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} & -\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-6{t}^{2}+\\frac{41}{3}t-8 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-\\frac{13}{3}t+5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(\\frac{5{a}_{1}-8{a}_{2}}{3}\\right){t}^{2}+\\left(\\frac{5{b}_{1}-8{b}_{2}}{3}\\right)t+\\left(\\frac{5{c}_{1}-8{c}_{2}}{3}\\right) \\\\ \\left(\\frac{-4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}}{3}\\right){t}^{2}+\\left(\\frac{-4{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}}{3}\\right)t+\\left(\\frac{-4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}}{3}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-6{t}^{2}+\\frac{41}{3}t-8 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-\\frac{13}{3}t+5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0{t}^{2}+2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 0{t}^{2}+2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(\\frac{5{a}_{1}-8{a}_{2}-18}{3}\\right){t}^{2}+\\left(\\frac{5{b}_{1}-8{b}_{2}+41}{3}\\right)t+\\left(\\frac{5{c}_{1}-8{c}_{2}-24}{3}\\right) \\\\ \\left(\\frac{-4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+9}{3}\\right){t}^{2}+\\left(\\frac{-4{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}-13}{3}\\right)t+\\left(\\frac{-4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+15}{3}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coefficients of like powers of $t,$ we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{a}_{1}-8{a}_{2}-18}{3}=0 \\\\ \\frac{-4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+9}{3}=0 \\\\ \\frac{5{b}_{1}-8{b}_{2}+41}{3}=2{a}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{-4{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}-13}{3}=2{a}_{2} \\\\ \\frac{5{c}_{1}-8{c}_{2}-24}{3}={b}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{-4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+15}{3}={b}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first and second equations, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=2,\\,{a}_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the second and third equations, and solving for ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=-1,\\,{b}_{2}=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these last two values into the fourth and sixth equations, and solving for ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=1,\\,{c}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{t}^{2}-t+1 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+3t-2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186564502 :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $3{a}_{1}-2{a}_{2}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent."] [17592186564502 :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-2{a}_{2} & =3\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -21\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [17592186564506 :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})<0$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})>0\n$$\n\nIII. The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is a local maximum of $f$"] [17592186564506 :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we obtain   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})=det({f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=(-1)^{2}-{0}^{2}=1>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})={({f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}))}_{11}=-1<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})>0$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})<0,\n$$ the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [17592186564519 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 2x-3\\end{bmatrix},\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $2x-y=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =3 \\\\ 2(0)-(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0+1 \\\\ -3-1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\notin L\n$$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =3 \\\\ 2(1)-(-4) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""] [17592186564527 :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(2,-1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(2,-1)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)>0$\n\nIII. The point $(2,-1)$ is a local minimum of $f$"] [17592186564527 :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(2,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(2,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(2,-1)=det({f}^{″}(2,-1))=(-4)\\cdot (-6)-{0}^{2}=24>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)=({f}^{″}(2,-1))_{11}=-4<0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(2,-1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)<0,$ the point $(2,-1)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186564529 :question/problem "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n$$\n\\displaystyle {a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186564529 :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}=-3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=-3{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-3{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."] [17592186564531 :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186564531 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}x & =\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}}^{{Q}^{T}}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{b} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=-9 \\\\ {x}_{2}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=9.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8(9) & =-9 \\\\ 9{x}_{1} & =-81 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186564532 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,-\n$$\n{{field-2}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186564532 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0\\cdot {e}^{2(0)} \\\\ {e}^{2(0)}\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-0}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}+1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=3 \\\\ {c}_{2}+1=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we have ${c}_{1}=3.$ From the second equation, we get ${c}_{2}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186565420 :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=-1-t+2{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t-{t}^{2},\\,q(t)=3-3t.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\},$ and $$\n\\displaystyle [q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$"] [17592186565420 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-1-t+2{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(t-{t}^{2}) & =3-3t \\\\ -{x}_{1}+(-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})t+(2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =3-3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{array}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{array}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6\\end{array}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations, we get ${x}_{1}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=-6.$ \n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle [q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3+(-6)=-9.$"] [17592186565421 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{B}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $$\n\\displaystyle [A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$"] [17592186565421 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 & {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=6 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{array}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{array}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=6 \\\\ {x}_{2}=4 \\\\ -{x}_{2}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=4.$ From the first equation, we get  ${x}_{1}=6.$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle [A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6+4=10.$"] [17592186565445 :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2-t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=3-2{t}^{2},\\,q(t)=3t-4{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\},$ and $$\n\\displaystyle [q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$"] [17592186565445 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2-t)+{x}_{2}(3-2{t}^{2}) & =3t-4{t}^{2} \\\\ (2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})-{x}_{1}t-2{x}_{2}{t}^{2} & =3t-4{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4\\end{array}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4\\end{array}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4\\end{array}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ From the  first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle [q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3+2=-1.$"] [17592186565478 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{B}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 9 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $$\n\\displaystyle [A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$"] [17592186565478 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 9 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2} \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2} & 3{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=-5 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{array}{cc|c}6 & 9 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{array}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}6 & 9 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{array}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}6 & 9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{9}{2} & -3\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+9\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} & =8 \\\\ 6{x}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle [A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}=1.\n$$"] [17592186566196 :multistep/context "A company produces two types of products, type A and type B. The revenue function of the company, in thousands of dollars, is given by $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-8{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-1,\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x={\\begin{bmatrix}\\,x & y\\,\\end{bmatrix}}^{T},\n$$ $x$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product A, and $y$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product B. The company can adjust the prices per unit for both types of products by adding or removing certain features."] [17592186566202 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following linearity properties for curl:\n\n- $∇\\times (kF)=k∇\\times F$\n\n- $∇\\times (F+G)=∇\\times F+∇\\times G$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (F-3G) & =∇\\times F+∇\\times (-3G) \\\\  & =∇\\times F-3∇\\times G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\times F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{z}+x & xy & {y}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2\\sqrt{z}+x)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\sqrt{z}+x)]k \\\\  & =(2y-0)i-(0-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}})j+(y-0)k \\\\  & =2y\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,j+y\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (F-3G) & =∇\\times F-3∇\\times G \\\\  & =2y\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,j+y\\,k-3[3{x}^{2}y\\,k] \\\\  & =2y\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,j+y\\,k-9{x}^{2}y\\,k \\\\  & =2y\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,j+y(1-9{x}^{2})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating at the point $(1,0,9),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\times (F-3G))(1,0,9) & =2(0)\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}}\\,j+0(1-9(1)^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186566204 :question/worked-solution "We computed the second partial derivatives in part 5. So, the second derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}24{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}-16 & 32xy \\\\ 32xy & 16{x}^{2}+12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n* Evaluating the second derivative at $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)=\\begin{bmatrix}32 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 44\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nH(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)=32\\cdot 44-0\\cdot 0=1\\,408.\n$$\n\n* Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=-16\\cdot 12-0\\cdot 0=-192.\n$$"] [17592186566209 :question/worked-solution "We shall use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\times (f\\,F)=f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇f\\times F.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f\\times F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ z & 1 & x \\\\ 3z & 2x & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[1(0)-x(2x)]i-[z(0)-x(3z)]j+[z(2x)-1(3z)]k \\\\  & =-2{x}^{2}\\,i+3xz\\,j+(2xz-3z)\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-2{x}^{2},3xz,2xz-3z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F) & =f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F \\\\  & =(xz+y)⟨0,3,2⟩+⟨-2{x}^{2},3xz,2xz-3z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,3(xz+y),2(xz+y)⟩+⟨-2{x}^{2},3xz,2xz-3z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{x}^{2},3(xz+y)+3xz,2(xz+y)+2xz-3z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{x}^{2},6xz+3y,4xz+2y-3z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F)(1,0,0) & =⟨-2(1)^{2},6(1)(0)+3(0),4(1)(0)+2(0)-3(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186566217 :question/worked-solution "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x & \\,16{x}^{2}y+12y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x=0 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}y+12y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}8x({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2)=0 \\\\ 4y(4{x}^{2}+3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $4{x}^{2}+3\\ne 0,$ from the second equation of the system, we obtain that\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ 8x({x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ 8x({x}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(0,0).$\n\nNote that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these points, they are also stationary points."] [17592186566221 :question/worked-solution "We shall use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\times (f\\,F)=f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇f\\times F.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f\\times F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {e}^{-y} & -x{e}^{-y} & 0 \\\\ x{e}^{y} & yz & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[(-x{e}^{-y})(0)-(yz)(0)]i-[({e}^{-y})(0)-(x{e}^{y})(0)]j+[({e}^{-y})(yz)-(x{e}^{y})(-x{e}^{-y})]k \\\\  & =0\\,i+0j+(yz{e}^{-y}+{x}^{2})k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,yz{e}^{-y}+{x}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F) & =f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F \\\\  & =(x{e}^{-y})⟨-y,0,-x{e}^{y}⟩+⟨0,0,yz{e}^{-y}+{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-xy{e}^{-y},0,-{x}^{2}⟩+⟨0,0,yz{e}^{-y}+{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-xy{e}^{-y},0,yz{e}^{-y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n∇\\times (f\\,F)(0,1,2)=⟨0,0,\\frac{2}{e}⟩.\n$$"] [17592186566224 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following linearity properties for curl:\n\n- $∇\\times (kF)=k∇\\times F$\n\n- $∇\\times (F+G)=∇\\times F+∇\\times G$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\times F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & 3z & -2y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3z)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3z)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)]k \\\\  & =(-2-3)i-(0-0)j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =-5\\,i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (F+G) & =∇\\times F+∇\\times G \\\\  & =-5\\,i+(6z-1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating at the point $(0,-1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\times (F+G))(0,-1,1) & =-5\\,i+(6(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =-5\\,i+5\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186566263 :question/worked-solution "For our scalar function, we have that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={(∇f)}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEarlier, we found that \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x.\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-8{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-1)=16{x}^{2}y+12y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x\\, & \\,16{x}^{2}y+12y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186566491 :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{bmatrix}$ is not invertible"] [17592186566583 :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has infinite solutions"] [17592186566635 :answer/value "the system $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has exactly one solution"] [17592186566759 :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{bmatrix}$ is singular"] [17592186567012 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-1,-4]\\times [1,4]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,4]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[-2.5,\\,2.5]^{T}.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{2}=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186567012 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2.5 \\\\ 2.5\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4(-2.5) & -2(2.5) \\\\ 2(-2.5) & 2(2.5)\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2(-2.5)^{2}-(2.5)^{2}-4 \\\\ (-2.5)^{2}+(2.5)^{2}-8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2.5 \\\\ 2.5\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2.25 \\\\ 4.5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2.5 \\\\ 2.5\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{(-10)\\cdot 5-(-5)\\cdot (-5)}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2.25 \\\\ 4.5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2.5 \\\\ 2.5\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{75}\\begin{bmatrix}33.75 \\\\ -33.75\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2.05 \\\\ 2.05\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186567017 :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If $$\n\\displaystyle det\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0,\n$$ then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$ .* \n\nI. The lines $ax+by=0$ and $cx+dy=0$ don't intersect\n\nII. The lines $ax+by=0$ and $cx+dy=0$ intersect at infinitely many points\n\nIII. The system $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}x=0\n$$ has the unique zero solution."] [17592186567017 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($det(A)\\ne 0$), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option III gives us a true statement: \n\n*If $$\n\\displaystyle det\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0,\n$$ then the system $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}x=0\n$$ has the unique zero solution.* $\\,✓$"] [17592186567023 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-3,-1]\\times [1,3]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}x{y}^{3}-y+7 \\\\ {x}^{3}-xy+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point $[-1,\\,2]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration, rounding your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$8$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$-2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$-2.095$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-4}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186567023 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{3}-y+7) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{3}-y+7) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-xy+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-xy+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{3} & 3x{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-y & -x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{3} & 3x{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-y & -x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{\\begin{bmatrix}x{y}^{3}-y+7 \\\\ {x}^{3}-xy+1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{3} & 3x{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-y & -x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}x{y}^{3}-y+7 \\\\ {x}^{3}-xy+1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{3} & 3(-1)(2)^{2}-1 \\\\ 3(-1)^{2}-2 & -(-1)\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}(-1)(2)^{3}-2+7 \\\\ (-1)^{3}-(-1)(2)+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}8 & -13 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{23}{21} \\\\ -\\frac{19}{21}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{23}{21} \\\\ -\\frac{19}{21}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{23}{21} \\\\ -\\frac{19}{21}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{44}{21} \\\\ \\frac{23}{21}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}-2.095 \\\\ 1.095\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [17592186567034 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nLet $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ If $k\\ne 2$, then\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{bmatrix}=4-2k\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is invertible and $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ is the unique solution of  $Ax=0.$\nAlso, the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nhas exactly one solution, and the lines $2x-y=1$ and $4x-ky=0$ intersect at exactly one point.  \n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If $k\\ne 2$, then the system $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has exactly one solution.* $\\,✓$"] [17592186567040 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0.5,1]\\times [1,1.5]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ x{y}^{3}-2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point $[1,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$].$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$3$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186567040 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as\n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{3}-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{3}-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6x & 2y \\\\ {y}^{3} & 3x{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}6x & 2y \\\\ {y}^{3} & 3x{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ x{y}^{3}-2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}6x & 2y \\\\ {y}^{3} & 3x{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ x{y}^{3}-1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6(1) & 2(1) \\\\ {1}^{3} & 3(1)(1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}3(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}-4 \\\\ (1)(1)^{3}-2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186567049 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."] [17592186567056 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $k=2$ then\n\n$$\ndet\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }k & 2\\end{bmatrix}=det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2=0,\n$$\n\nbut  according to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."] [17592186567058 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-3,-1]\\times [1,3]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{5}-y+10 \\\\ xy-{y}^{2}+3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point $[-1,\\,1]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration, rounding your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$5$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$-8$\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$0.786$\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186567058 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{5}-y+10) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{5}-y+10) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-{y}^{2}+3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-{y}^{2}+3)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{x}^{4} & -1 \\\\ y & x-2y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}5{x}^{4} & -1 \\\\ y & x-2y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{5}-y+10 \\\\ xy-{y}^{2}+3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}5{x}^{4} & -1 \\\\ y & x-2y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{5}-y+10 \\\\ xy-{y}^{2}+3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5(-1)^{4} & -1 \\\\ 1 & (-1)-2(1)\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}(-1)^{5}-(1)+10 \\\\ (-1)(1)-{1}^{2}+3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{23}{14} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{14}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{23}{14} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{14}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{23}{14} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{14}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{37}{14} \\\\ \\frac{11}{14}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}-2.643 \\\\ 0.786\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [17592186567063 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,2]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}y-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}y+{y}^{4}-4x+2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$1.497$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186567063 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}y-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}y-3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}y+{y}^{4}-4x+2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y+{y}^{4}-4x+2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}^{3}+2xy & -3{y}^{2}+{x}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}y-4 & {x}^{3}+4{y}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}^{3}+2xy & -3{y}^{2}+{x}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}y-4 & {x}^{3}+4{y}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}y-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}y+{y}^{4}-4x+2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}^{3}+2xy & -3{y}^{2}+{x}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}y-4 & {x}^{3}+4{y}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}y-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}y+{y}^{4}-4x+2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}}. \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4(1)^{3}+2(1)(2) & -3(2)^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\ 3(1)^{2}(2)-4 & {1}^{3}+4(2)^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{4}-{2}^{3}+(1)^{2}(2)-3 \\\\ (1)^{3}(2)+{2}^{4}-4(1)+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -11 \\\\ 2 & 33\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}. \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{8\\cdot 33-(-11)\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}33 & 11 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{286}\\begin{bmatrix}-88 \\\\ 144\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}1.308 \\\\ 1.497\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [17592186567067 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-3,-2]\\times [-3,0]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10 \\\\ {y}^{3}-3x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[-2,\\,-2]^{T}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$-1.967$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186567067 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-3x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}-3x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 2y \\\\ -3 & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 2y \\\\ -3 & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10 \\\\ {y}^{3}-3x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 2y \\\\ -3 & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10 \\\\ {y}^{3}-3x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2(-2) & 2(-2) \\\\ -3 & 3(-2)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}-10 \\\\ (-2)^{3}-3(-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 12\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot 12-(-4)\\cdot (-3)}\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{60}\\begin{bmatrix}-32 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{38}{15} \\\\ -\\frac{59}{30}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}-2.533 \\\\ -1.967\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [17592186567070 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,3]\\times [-2,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-xy-4 \\\\ x-{y}^{3}-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[2,\\,0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[1,\\,-2]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{2}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186567070 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-xy-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-xy-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-{y}^{3}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-{y}^{3}-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x-y & -x \\\\ 1 & -3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2x-y & -x \\\\ 1 & -3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-xy-4 \\\\ x-{y}^{3}-1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2x-y & -x \\\\ 1 & -3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-xy-4 \\\\ x-{y}^{3}-1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2(1)-(-2) & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3(-2)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2}-(1)(-2)-4 \\\\ 1-(-2)^{3}-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -12\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-12)-(-1)\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{47}\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 33\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{67}{47} \\\\ -\\frac{61}{47}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}1.426 \\\\ -1.298\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [17592186567074 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."] [17592186567076 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,0]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,1]^{T}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$-0.769$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186567076 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-{x}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}-{x}^{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2} & -2y \\\\ -2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2} & -2y \\\\ -2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2} & -2y \\\\ -2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}3(-1)^{2} & -2(1) \\\\ -2(-1) & 3(1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}(-1)^{3}-{1}^{2}+1 \\\\ {1}^{3}-(-1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 3-2\\cdot (-2)}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{10}{13} \\\\ \\frac{11}{13}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}-0.769 \\\\ 0.846\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."]}
